<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Product Strategy Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Organizations can struggle to create effective product strategies when
                    priorities are unclear, customer insights are limited, or product
                    decisions are disconnected from business objectives. Addressing these
                    challenges creates a stronger foundation for product growth.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Unclear Product Direction</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Without a clear product vision, teams may work on disconnected
                    initiatives. A well-defined strategic direction helps teams understand
                    what the product should achieve and where resources should be focused.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Limited Customer Understanding</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Strategic decisions based on assumptions can result in products that
                    fail to address important customer problems. Customer research and
                    product analytics provide stronger evidence for strategic decisions.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Too Many Priorities</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Attempting to pursue every opportunity can spread resources too thinly.
                    A structured prioritization process helps teams focus on initiatives
                    that provide the greatest value.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Weak Competitive Differentiation</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Products may struggle to stand out when their value and differentiation
                    are unclear. Competitive research helps identify opportunities to
                    create a stronger market position.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Strategy and Execution Misalignment</b></h5>
                <p>
                    A product strategy has limited value when development teams are not
                    aligned with it. Connecting strategy to roadmaps, backlogs, releases,
                    and measurable outcomes improves execution.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Changing Market Conditions</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Customer expectations, competitors, technology, and market conditions
                    can change quickly. Product strategies should remain flexible and be
                    reviewed regularly.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
